# security.txt template (RFC 9116). Normally you create this file with:
#   python scripts/make_security_txt.py --client client.json --out security.txt
# If you fill it in by hand, replace the <...> parts, delete fields you do not use,
# then check it with: python scripts/make_security_txt.py --check security.txt
# Publish it at https://<domain>/.well-known/security.txt (HTTPS, plain text, UTF-8).
#
# Required. One or more ways to report a problem: mailto:, https:// or tel:
Contact: mailto:security@<domain>
Contact: https://<domain>/security/report
# Required, exactly once. Date and time in UTC. Keep it less than a year ahead and renew it in time.
Expires: <YYYY-MM-DD>T23:59:59Z
# Optional. Where your public PGP key is (a link, never the key itself).
Encryption: https://<domain>/.well-known/pgp-key.txt
# Optional. A page thanking people who reported vulnerabilities.
Acknowledgments: https://<domain>/security/thanks
# Optional. Languages you can handle reports in, comma-separated.
Preferred-Languages: en
# Recommended. The address where this file is published.
Canonical: https://<domain>/.well-known/security.txt
# Recommended. Your vulnerability disclosure policy.
Policy: https://<domain>/security/policy
# Optional. Security job openings.
# Hiring: https://<domain>/jobs
# Optional. Machine-readable security advisories (CSAF provider metadata).
# CSAF: https://<domain>/.well-known/csaf/provider-metadata.json
